# States of Matter and the Gas Laws: How Particles Fill Their World

> Matter organises itself into states — solid, liquid, gas, and the
> exotic plasma — by balancing the pull of intermolecular attraction
> against the energy of motion. Gases are the simplest state to
> describe mathematically, and their laws powered the industrial
> revolution's steam engines.

## 1. The Three Familiar States

| Property | Solid | Liquid | Gas |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Shape | Fixed | Takes container's shape | Fills container |
| Volume | Fixed | Fixed | Fills container |
| Particle motion | Vibrate in place | Slide past each other | Fast, random, straight lines |
| Compressibility | Almost none | Very little | Large |
| Typical density | High | Medium | Low |

[definition]
A **state of matter** is a distinct arrangement of particles set by
the balance between intermolecular forces (pulling particles together)
and thermal energy (driving them apart). Heat a solid and it melts;
heat a liquid and it vaporises — each transition marks thermal energy
winning a round against attraction.
[/definition]

Melting, freezing, vaporisation, condensation, and sublimation (solid
straight to gas, as with dry ice or camphor) are **physical changes**:
the identity of the particles never changes, only their arrangement.

## 2. Intermolecular Forces: The Glue Behind the States

- **London dispersion forces** — momentary dipoles; exist between all
  molecules, dominate in non-polar ones (waxes, iodine).
- **Dipole–dipole forces** — permanent dipoles align plus-to-minus
  (HCl, acetone).
- **Hydrogen bonding** — the strongest common intermolecular force,
  when H is bonded to N, O, or F. It explains water's high boiling
  point, ice floating on water, and DNA's base pairing.

[tip]
Compare neighbours in the periodic table: HCl (−85 °C bp) vs H₂O
(+100 °C) vs H₂S (−60 °C). Water boils 185 °C higher than H₂S despite
similar molar mass — the fingerprint of extensive hydrogen bonding.
[/tip]

## 3. Pressure: Gases Push Back

Gas molecules collide with container walls billions of times a
second; the average push per area is **pressure**.

@math label="eq:pressure"
P = \frac{F}{A}
@/math

1 atmosphere (atm) = 101,325 Pa = 760 mm of mercury — the reading of a
barometer at sea level, where a column of mercury ~76 cm balances the
entire atmosphere's weight.

## 4. Boyle's Law: Volume vs Pressure (Constant T)

Squeezing a gas at fixed temperature crowds the same molecules into
less space — more wall collisions, higher pressure:

@math label="eq:boyle"
P_1V_1 = P_2V_2
@/math

A bubble of air rising from a diver at 40 m (5 atm) to the surface
(1 atm) expands to five times its volume — why divers must exhale
while ascending and never hold their breath.

## 5. Charles's Law: Volume vs Temperature (Constant P)

Heating a gas at fixed pressure makes particles hit harder, so the
piston rises until pressure rebalances:

@math label="eq:charles"
\frac{V_1}{T_1} = \frac{V_2}{T_2}
@/math

[warning]
The temperatures in Charles's law (and the ideal gas law) must be in
kelvin — $T(K) = T(°C) + 273.15$. Doubling 25 °C does not double the
gas volume; doubling 298 K does.
[/warning]

Extrapolating real gases' volume vs temperature towards zero pressure
points to the same limit for every gas: **absolute zero, −273.15 °C**
— the temperature at which, classically, particle motion ceases.

## 6. The Ideal Gas Equation

Combine the separate laws and count the molecules (in moles $n$) and
you reach the master equation:

@math label="eq:ideal-gas"
PV = nRT
@/math

with $R = 8.314$ J·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹. One mole of *any* ideal gas at standard
conditions occupies about 22.4 litres — a fact worth remembering.

@chart line title="Pressure of a fixed amount of gas at constant volume rises linearly with kelvin temperature (arbitrary units)"
labels:
100
200
300
400
500

series:
  - name: "P"
    data: 8 17 25 34 42
@/chart

[definition]
An **ideal gas** has particles with negligible volume and no
intermolecular attractions. Real gases approach this at high
temperature and low pressure; near condensation they deviate and the
corrections of van der Waals' equation matter.
[/definition]

## 7. Partial Pressures (Dalton's Law)

In a mixture, each gas pushes independently:

@math label="eq:dalton"
P_{total} = P_a + P_b + P_c + \dots
@/math

Air is ~78% N₂, 21% O₂, 1% Ar — so at 1 atm, oxygen's partial
pressure is ~0.21 atm. This is why high-altitude air feels "thin":
total pressure is lower, so the partial pressure driving oxygen into
your blood is lower too, even though the percentages are unchanged.

## 8. Plasma: The Fourth State

Heat a gas past ~10,000 K and electrons tear free from their atoms —
the result is **plasma**, a soup of ions and electrons that conducts
electricity and responds to magnetic fields.

- Over 99% of the visible universe is plasma: stars, nebulae,
  lightning, and the Sun's corona.
- Neon signs and plasma TVs confine glowing plasma in tubes.
- Fusion research (ITER and its cousins) aims to bottle star-grade
  plasma in magnetic fields — the pursuit of limitless clean energy.

[summary]
Solids, liquids, and gases differ in how strongly intermolecular
forces pin particles in place, with hydrogen bonding the strongest
common glue. Gases obey simple laws: Boyle ($P \propto 1/V$ at
constant T), Charles ($V \propto T$ in kelvin), combined in
$PV = nRT$, with partial pressures adding independently (Dalton).
Absolute zero (−273.15 °C) is the extrapolated zero of particle
motion, and beyond gases lies plasma — the ionised state that fills
the universe and may one day power it.
[/summary]
